                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 0,
                "content": "First ten minutes, honestly:\n\nWhat I expected: I open the app once, it's sitting there in the menu bar, and I never think about it again. My address, my card, my PAN \u2014 all just there when some form wants them. That's the whole point for me. I fill a lot of forms and I'm tired of retyping my address.\n\nWhat I looked for: the shortcut. I pressed Control+Option+V almost immediately, because the only thing I care about is that it works inside whatever app I'm already in \u2014 a checkout page, a booking site, a form someone sent me.\n\nWhat I tried: searched for \"address\" in the palette, hit Return, and it dropped into the field. That part was great. No copying, no switching windows. Then I searched for my card, to keep it handy for a quick checkout, and it asked for Touch ID. Fine the first time. But it seems to want it again after I walk away and come back, and I don't want my thumb on the sensor every time I just want a card number I already own.\n\nAlso, I closed the window with the Saved badge before I really read it. I assumed it saved. It probably did, but I couldn't tell you what the badge was actually telling me.\n\nWhere I stopped: at the \"protected\" thing. It seals fields and asks for a second key, and I didn't go digging in settings to work out what that second key is or how to unseal what I've already sealed. I just backed out.\n\nWhat it should have told me, at each step: right at the start, one line \u2014 \"This vault stays unlocked while you're logged in; protected items will ask for Touch ID each time after your screen locks.\" In the palette, tell me what the sealed count means before I click it, not after. When something gets sealed, tell me what I'll need later to open it. And don't make me find that in settings, because I won't.\n\nNice idea, and the typing-into-any-field part is exactly what I wanted. The protected/settings side lost me.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:48:28.874526",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 3,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 1,
                "content": "Ten minutes in, read as a threat model, because that is how I read everything:\n\n0:00 \u2014 \"Encrypted on this Mac\" + \"opens at launch without a prompt\" tells me the vault key lives in my login keychain, i.e. readable by anything running as me. That is an acceptable baseline, but the copy should say it. \"No prompt\" is not a feature, it is a key custody decision, and I want it on the security page, not inferred.\n\n0:02 \u2014 I went looking for the security page before I put anything in. Two things are missing. First: \"sealed under a second key\" \u2014 where does that key live? Secure Enclave, a password I never set, or a file next to the vault? Touch ID is an authentication gesture, not key material, and those are very different threat models. Second: \"the palette shows a sealed count and unlocks them with Touch ID.\" That reads as unlock-all. So does one Touch ID for one lookup leave every sealed field readable for the rest of the session? I tried unlocking one and then searching for another sealed field. Whichever way that lands, the Touch ID dialog should say it in one sentence: \"unlocking reveals all N sealed items until the vault is sealed again.\"\n\n0:05 \u2014 \"Marked for agents\" prints non-sensitive fields as JSON. The entire security story of the feature rests on that word, and I cannot see the classifier. Is a protected item excluded from the agent export automatically, or is \"non-sensitive\" a per-field flag I can set wrong? I marked an item protected and then exported it. If that succeeded, the word is doing work the code is not.\n\n0:10 \u2014 \"Search a field, press Return, and the value is typed into the focused field.\" Great for adoption, bad for leaks: the value lands wherever focus is, and \"whatever app you are in\" includes terminals, chat boxes and anything with autosave. There is no masking at the destination, no undo, and the destination's own history now has it. Same worry about \"editors autosave and show a Saved badge\" \u2014 saved where, in what form, while unlocked? If the file is structured plaintext-under-a-live-key on disk, \"encrypted\" is true and misleading at once.\n\nWhere I stopped: \"Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only.\" So unprotected records stay readable on a locked Mac. That means lock is not a boundary for most of my data \u2014 only for the items I remembered to seal. I stopped there because I could not tell my posture from the product's words, only from its behaviour, and the behaviour is only discoverable by trying to break it.\n\nWhat it should have shown me in the first minute: one page with the seal/unseal matrix \u2014 what is sealed on sleep, on lock, on sign-out, on quit, on agent export, on keychain access by another process \u2014 and what \"opens without a prompt\" costs me. Then I would trust it enough to put real records in it. Until then I test with fake ones.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:58:35.404986",
                "score": 0
            }
        ]
    }
]
