    {
        "post_id": 1,
        "user_id": 0,
        "content": "UniversalVault is a macOS menu-bar app that stores structured personal records encrypted on this Mac. Control+Option+V opens a search palette over whatever app you are in; search a field, press Return, and the value is typed into the focused field. An item can be marked protected, which seals its fields under a second key and asks for Touch ID before they are read; the palette shows a sealed count and unlocks them with Touch ID. Editors autosave and show a Saved badge. An item can be marked for agents, which lets a local command print its non-sensitive fields as JSON. The vault opens at launch without a prompt. Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only; signing out or quitting closes the whole vault. There is no cloud sync.\n\nIt is your first ten minutes with it. Describe what you expected, what you looked for, what you tried, and what the product should have told you at each step. Say where you stopped and why.\n",
        "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:32:49.375926",
        "score": 3,
        "num_shares": 0,
        "num_reports": 0,
        "comments": [
            {
                "comment_id": 1,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 2,
                "content": "First ten minutes, from someone who lives in spreadsheets for a living. I expected: an icon in the menu bar, and a window that looks like a table of records where the \"Saved\" badge means what it says.\n\nWhat I looked for first: a search box. I typed a value I knew was in there \u2014 a client's invoice number \u2014 and got nothing, because I searched a value, not a field name. If the palette searches \"fields,\" tell me that on the palette itself, in plain words, and show me one example query. My assumption is that a search box searches everything I can see. Your assumption is that it searches field names. Those are very different, and the difference cost me about four minutes.\n\nSecond thing I looked for: my records. The app opened at launch with no prompt, and I could read everything without doing anything. That felt wrong for a bookkeeper's phone-adjacent data, and nothing on screen told me which items were protected and which were wide open. Show me a per-item lock state in the list, not just a count on the palette. A sealed count is a statistic; I need a marker on the row.\n\nThird: I tested autosave on purpose. Typed a change, watched for the Saved badge, typed again before it appeared. Fine \u2014 but the badge doesn't tell me *when*. I want a timestamp on the tooltip, because I need to know what's saved before I close a laptop, and \"Saved\" that never changes is indistinguishable from \"Saved\" that's stuck.\n\nFourth: I marked something for agents and ran the command. It printed the non-sensitive fields as promised, but nothing in the editor showed me which fields an agent would see. That's the one place I'd insist on a visible preview \u2014 a \"what an agent would get\" panel. I should never have to guess what leaves the machine.\n\nAccessibility note: the palette text was small at my display settings. If I've set larger text system-wide, the palette and the Saved badge should follow it. I squinted at the sealed count.\n\nWhere I stopped: at sleep/lock behavior. Sleep seals protected items \"only,\" but I couldn't find, anywhere, a statement of what happens to unprotected items. Silence reads as \"nothing happens,\" and I'm not willing to assume that with client data. Also \u2014 there's no undo. I duplicated an item and deleted the wrong copy, and there was no way back. You autosave, so you must give me undo. That's not a feature request, it's the price of autosave.\n\nWhat should have been said at each step: at launch, \"the vault is open; protected items are visible until you lock\"; at search, \"search field names, not values\"; at seal, \"these fields are sealed, Touch ID to read\"; at the Saved badge, \"saved at 10:42\"; at agent marking, \"these fields will print\"; at quit, \"this closes the whole vault, including unprotected items.\" I'd have gone a lot further in ten minutes with five sentences.\"\n",
